You can use the following JavaScript function to generate prime numbers from 1 to n:

```javascript
function getPrimes(n) {
    const primes = [];
    let isPrime;

    for(let i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
        isPrime = true;
        for(let j = 2; j < i; j++) {
            if(i % j === 0) {
                isPrime = false;
                break;
            }
        }
        if(isPrime) {
            primes.push(i);
        }
    }
    return primes;
}

getPrimes(10); // [2, 3, 5, 7]
```

In this code, we start by declaring an empty array called `primes` to store the prime numbers. We then initialize the variable `isPrime` to track whether a number is prime or not.

We use a nested loop to iterate through each number from 2 to n. The outer loop iterates over each number, while the inner loop checks if the number is divisible by any number from 2 to itself minus 1. If it is divisible, we set `isPrime` to false and break out of the inner loop.

If the number is not divisible by any number in the inner loop, it means the number is prime, and we push it to the `primes` array.

Finally, we return the `primes` array containing all the prime numbers from 1 to n.

When we call `getPrimes(10)`, it will return the array `[2, 3, 5, 7]`, as these are the prime numbers between 1 and 10.

