```cpp
void merge_sort(int arr[], int len) {
    if (len < 2) return;
    int mid = len / 2;
    int left[mid], right[len - mid];
    for (int i = 0; i < mid; i++) {
        left[i] = arr[i];
    }
    for (int i = mid; i < len; i++) {
        right[i - mid] = arr[i];
    }
    merge_sort(left, mid);
    merge_sort(right, len - mid);
    merge(arr, left, right, mid, len - mid);
}

void merge(int arr[], int left[], int right[], int left_length, int right_length) {
    int i = 0, j = 0, k = 0;
    while (i < left_length && j < right_length) {
        if (left[i] < right[j]) {
            arr[k] = left[i];
            i++;
        } else {
            arr[k] = right[j];
            j++;
        }
        k++;
    }
    while (i < left_length) {
        arr[k] = left[i];
        k++;
        i++;
    }
    while (j < right_length) {
        arr[k] = right[j];
        k++;
        j++;
    }
}
```

The `merge_sort` function takes an array `arr` and its length `len` as input. It recursively divides the array into two halves until it reaches the base case of having a single element or an empty array. It then merges the divided arrays using the `merge` function.

The `merge` function takes the original array `arr`, the two divided arrays `left` and `right`, and their respective lengths `left_length` and `right_length` as input. It merges the divided arrays by comparing the elements at corresponding indices and placing them in the correct order in the original array.

By recursively dividing the array and merging the divided parts in a sorted order, the merge sort algorithm achieves an average runtime complexity of O(n log n).

