```python
def bubble_sort(nums):
    for i in range(len(nums)-1):
        for j in range(0, len(nums)-i-1):
            if nums[j] > nums[j+1]:
                nums[j], nums[j+1] = nums[j+1], nums[j]
    return nums
```

In this implementation, we use two nested loops. The outer loop iterates from the first element to the second-to-last element of the list, while the inner loop iterates from the first element to the element before the last unsorted element.

Within the inner loop, we compare each pair of adjacent elements. If the current element is greater than the next element, we swap them using tuple assignment. This process continues until the largest element "bubbles" up to its correct position at the end of the list. 

After completing the inner loop for each pass of the outer loop, the largest unsorted element is placed in its correct position. This process is repeated until the entire list is sorted in ascending order.

Finally, the sorted list is returned as the output of the `bubble_sort` function.

