One way to achieve this is by using an algorithm that dynamically resizes the array whenever it reaches its capacity. Here's an example implementation in Java:

```java
import java.util.Arrays;

public class Stack {
    private int capacity;
    private int top = -1;
    private int[] buffer;

    public Stack(int capacity) {
        this.capacity = capacity;
        buffer = new int[capacity];
    }

    public void push(int value) {
        if (top == capacity - 1) {
            capacity *= 2;
            buffer = Arrays.copyOf(buffer, capacity);
        }
        buffer[++top] = value;
    }

    public int pop() {
        if (top == -1) {
            System.out.println("Stack is empty");
            return -1;
        } else {
            int value = buffer[top--];
            return value;
        }
    }

    public int peek() {
        if (top == -1) {
            System.out.println("Stack is empty");
            return -1;
        } else {
            int value = buffer[top];
            return value;
        }
    }

    public int size() {
        return top + 1;
    }

    public void printStack() {
        for (int i = top; i >= 0; i--) {
            System.out.println(buffer[i]);
        }
    }
}
```

In this implementation, the `Stack` class has a private `capacity` variable to keep track of the current capacity of the stack. The `top` variable represents the index of the top element in the stack, initialized to -1 when the stack is empty. The `buffer` array is used to store the elements of the stack.

To push an element onto the stack, the `push` method is used. If the `top` variable reaches the `capacity - 1`, indicating that the stack is full, the `capacity` is doubled by using the `Arrays.copyOf` method to create a new array with double the capacity, and the elements from the old array are copied into the new array.

To pop an element from the stack, the `pop` method is used. If the `top` variable is -1, indicating that the stack is empty, an error message is printed and -1 is returned. Otherwise, the value at the top index is retrieved and the `top` is decremented.

The `peek` method is similar to the `pop` method, but it does not remove the element from the stack. It returns the value at the top index if the stack is not empty, otherwise it prints an error message and returns -1.

The `size` method returns the number of elements in the stack, which is calculated by adding 1 to the `top` index.

The `printStack` method is used to print the elements of the stack in LIFO (last-in, first-out) order.

Overall, this algorithm allows you to implement an arbitrary sized stack using an array of fixed size in Java.

