To achieve the desired pagination format, you can use the following code:

```go
package main

import (
    "fmt"
)

func main() {
    pages(8, 2, 13)
}

func pages(n int, around int, count int) {
    first := n - around
    if first < 1 {
        first = 1
    }
    last := n + around
    if last > count {
        last = count
    }

    if first > 1 {
        for i := 1; i <= 2 && i < first; i++ {
            fmt.Println(i)
        }
        if 3 < first {
            fmt.Println("...")
        }
    }

    for i := first; i <= last; i++ {
        fmt.Println(i)
    }

    if last < count {
        if last <= count-3 {
            fmt.Println("...")
        }
        end := count - 1
        if end <= last {
            end = last + 1
        }
        for i := end; i <= count; i++ {
            fmt.Println(i)
        }

    }
}
```

This code takes three parameters: `n` (the current page number), `around` (the number of pages to display before and after the current page), and `count` (the total number of pages). The function `pages` calculates the first and last page numbers to display based on the current page. It then loops through the pages and prints them accordingly.

To improve the code, you can make the number of "prefix" and "suffix" pages variable. Here is an updated version of the code:

```go
package main

import (
    "fmt"
)

func main() {
    pages(10, 3, 20)
}

func pages(n int, around int, count int) {
    var i int
    for i = 1; i <= 2 && i<=count; i++ {
        fmt.Println(i)
    }

    if i < n-around {
        fmt.Println("...")
        i = n - around
    }

    for ; i <= n+around && i<=count; i++ {
        fmt.Println(i)
    }

    if i < count-1 {
        fmt.Println("...")
        i = count - 1
    }

    for ; i <= count; i++ {
        fmt.Println(i)
    }
}
```

This version uses a single loop and simplifies the code by avoiding unnecessary checks. It follows a natural logic of how a human would write down the pagination. The caller only needs to focus on rendering the current page or separator.

If you want to customize the rendering of each page, you can use a callback function. Here is an example:

```go
package main

import (
    "fmt"
)

func main() {
    pages(10, 3, 20, func(i int) {
        if i < 0 {
            fmt.Println("...")
            return
        }
        fmt.Println(i)
    })
}

func pages(n int, around int, count int, render func(int)) {
    var i int
    for i = 1; i <= 2 && i <= count; i++ {
        render(i)
    }

    if i < n-around {
        render(-1)
        i = n - around
    }

    for ; i <= n+around && i <= count; i++ {
        render(i)
    }

    if i < count-1 {
        render(-1)
        i = count - 1
    }

    for ; i <= count; i++ {
        render(i)
    }
}
```

In this version, the `pages` function takes an additional parameter `render`, which is a callback function. The `render` function is called for each page, allowing you to customize the rendering as needed.

Overall, these solutions provide a flexible and efficient way to achieve the desired pagination format in Go.

