To reverse a string without using any built-in string manipulation methods or additional data structures in Java, we can use the following algorithm:

1. Convert the string to a character array. This is necessary because strings in Java are immutable, so we cannot directly modify the characters in place.

2. Use two pointers, one pointing to the start of the character array (i.e., the first character) and the other pointing to the end of the character array (i.e., the last character).

3. Swap the characters at the two pointer positions and move the pointers towards each other until they meet in the middle.

4. Convert the character array back to a string using the `new String()` constructor.

Here's the implementation of the algorithm:

```java
public class ReverseString {
    public static String reverse(String str) {
        // Convert the string to a character array
        char[] chars = str.toCharArray();

        // Use two pointers to swap characters
        int start = 0;
        int end = chars.length - 1;
        while (start < end) {
            // Swap characters at the two pointer positions
            char temp = chars[start];
            chars[start] = chars[end];
            chars[end] = temp;

            // Move the pointers towards each other
            start++;
            end--;
        }

        // Convert the character array back to a string
        return new String(chars);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String str = "Hello, World!";
        String reversed = reverse(str);
        System.out.println(reversed);
    }
}
```

This algorithm has a time complexity of O(n) because we need to iterate over the entire character array once, where n is the length of the string. The space complexity is O(1) because we are not using any additional data structures, only modifying the characters in the original character array.

